  @@ -1312,6 +1312,47 @@ With the ``--flatten`` option columns will be created using ``topkey_nextkey`` c
  +.. _cli_inserting_data_key:
  +
  +Importing records held under a top-level key
  +--------------------------------------------
  +
  +Some JSON files keep their records in a list stored under a key of a top-level object, next to other keys. Consider this document, in a file called ``alaska.json``:
  +
  +.. code-block:: json
  +
  +    {
  +      "Version": "5.5.52.6",
  +      "List": [
  +        { "Description": "Nonpartisan", "Id": 1, "ExternalId": "" },
  +        { "Description": "Undeclared", "Id": 2, "ExternalId": "" }
  +      ]
  +    }
  +
  +By default a top-level object is inserted as a single row - here a row with a ``Version`` column and a ``List`` column holding the array as JSON text.
  +
  +Use ``--key`` to name the top-level key that holds the records:
  +
  +.. code-block:: bash
  +
  +    sqlite-utils insert data.db records alaska.json --key List
  +
  +This inserts two rows, with the columns ``Description``, ``Id`` and ``ExternalId``. The other top-level keys, ``Version`` here, are ignored. If the value of the key is an object it is inserted as a single row, and an empty list inserts no rows. A missing key, or a key whose value is a string, number, boolean or null, is an error, as is using ``--key`` when the top level of the JSON is an array. ``--key`` looks up a top-level key by its literal name: there is no support for nested paths.
  +
  +Or use ``--auto-key`` to have ``sqlite-utils`` find the key for you:
  +
  +.. code-block:: bash
  +
  +    sqlite-utils insert data.db records alaska.json --auto-key
  +
  +``--auto-key`` uses the one top-level key whose value is a non-empty list of objects. It is an error if no top-level key holds a list of objects, and it is also an error if more than one does - the error message lists every such key, so you can pick one with ``--key``. If the top level of the JSON is already an array it is imported as usual.
  +
  +Both options work with ``sqlite-utils insert``, ``upsert``, ``bulk`` and ``memory``, and can be combined with ``--flatten``, which is applied to the extracted rows. They cannot be used together, and they only apply to JSON input, so they cannot be combined with ``--nl``, ``--csv``, ``--tsv``, ``--lines`` or ``--text``. With ``sqlite-utils memory`` they apply to every JSON input of the call, while CSV, TSV and newline-delimited JSON inputs are imported as usual:
  +
  +.. code-block:: bash
  +
  +    sqlite-utils memory alaska.json "select * from alaska" --key List
  +
